LED 2026-11-03 (a) on each call, as recommended (coordinator-2026-11-03 D-004). Asked, the owner chose Q1 (a) over moving the evidence column forward, the route the interface rows took under OI-67.\n4282-\n4283-Filed by headless leg 31 (loop session 20261103T054112Z-32c0bd) under the owner's 2026-10-25 ruling that a decision only the owner can make, and that holds a row, is a pending open item its row's needs cite (coordinator-2026-11-03 D-002).\n4284-\n4285-WHAT IS TRUE. WI-992 had planner sub-agents re-draft P8's three rows under OI-120's ruling; they are filed as WI-994 (the test-file TC tag on the Implements: line and its one reader), WI-995 (the review test set derived from the row's SRs through the tags, refusing an empty or partial set and a code-touching row naming no SR) and WI-996 (the strict-mark check and the required [bars] red_mark key). Each owes its scope critique. Each drafter named one call it judged beyond OI-120.\n4286-\n4287-WHAT IS HELD. WI-994, WI-995 and WI-996: their claims, not their scope critiques.\"\"\"\n4288-blast_radius = \"\"\"P8's three rows only. Q2 also reaches adopters mid-DevStg-Impl (a row under an SR waits for every sibling LLR's failing test); Q3 a shipped step and setting.\"\"\"\n4289-options = \"\"\"Each call is ruled separately; (a) is the drafter's proposal.\n4290-- Q1 INTERIM TWO HOMES (WI-994). Until item 5 generates the TC evidence column from the tags, the tags and the evidence cells state the same fact. (a) Check them equal (a strict finding), the check retired when item 5 lands. (b) Freeze the cells and treat the tags as authoritative for WI-995 alone. FOR (a): no silent disagreement. AGAINST (a): ~1970 node tags must agree before the check goes strict.\n- **Consolidate, don't duplicate — the 0→A→B rule.** Edit-conservatively (agent\r\n  guide, \"Working agreement\") is scoped to the task in front of you: smallest\r\n  diff, within that one change. Across the codebase the goal is the opposite —\r\n  prefer the change that minimizes **total** behavior. When a fix wants the\r\n  same code in two places, don't patch each site: extract the shared stage\r\n  both sites call through, so two independent paths to a result (0→B, 0→D)\r\n  become one shared stage feeding both (0→A→B, 0→A→D). Where two or more\r\n  existing outputs already overlap, restructure so each behavior has exactly\r\n  one home — never an original plus a near-copy. The same principle governs\r\n  validation and error handling: implement once, at the boundary that owns the\r\n  behavior, never re-derived at each caller — this is the antidote skill's\r\n  \"validate once at the boundary\" applied at repo scale (vendored at\r\n  `skills/antidote/`, a per-fix companion to this repo-scale doctrine).\r\n- **When a guard is owed.** A guard is owed only where the input crosses a\r\n  trust boundary — a file on disk, the network, a person, another process, or a\r\n  model's output — or where the operation is irreversible or exposed to attack.\r\n  Data this code produced in-process is trusted: fix the producer instead. Two\r\n  consequences: the registries are hand-edited files on disk, so validating\r\n  them is owed; and a model's output is a boundary, so validating structured\r\n  output is owed.\r\n- **Record a decision where it is made.** A decision a later step depends on\r\n  is recorded by the step that makes it, in the commit where it is made, and\r\n  checked against that commit's parent. A later reader trusts the record and\r\n  never recomputes the decision from history or from current state (owner\r\n  ruling 2026-10-11).\r\n- **A structural move is its own commit.** A behaviour-preserving move\r\n  (a rename, an extraction, a file split) lands in a commit that changes no\r\n  behaviour, before the change it enables. Each diff is then reviewable as one\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
